// redundant_permit.dw with the redundant rule deleted.
//
// The pair is the point. `vacuity.py` reports the gated permit in redundant_permit.dw as
// REDUNDANT; this file is what you get by acting on that advice. Diffing the two confirms the
// advice was safe:
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/redundant_permit.dw \
//         --against tests/policies/redundant_permit_minimal.dw
//     ==> no difference
//
// The two findings check each other. "Deleting this rule changes no verdict" and "these two files
// decide identically" are the same claim approached from opposite ends, so a disagreement between
// them would mean one of the two is wrong.
//
// Note what the vocabulary has to do here: this file never mentions `Approve`, and the other one
// does. The checker takes the union of both files' actions, so Approve is still attempted. Without
// that it would report "no difference" having never tried the action the two might disagree on --
// the one wrong answer a diff must not give.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);
